From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 16:38:12 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] seam: hold IControlCore in ControlInteractivity

Flips _core from com_ptr<ControlCore> to the projected IControlCore, so
the constructor's engine switch is now the only place in the class that
names a concrete engine. This completes the Phase 5 prerequisite: a
ghostty pane can receive mouse and selection input once an engine exists
to bind there.

The UIA cluster (AttachUiaEngine/DetachUiaEngine/GetRenderData) stays on
the concrete type by design - it trades in pointers to WT's own renderer
objects, which is the coupling the interface exists to remove. It is
reached through a new _cascadiaCore(), which QIs for the ControlCore
*class* interface rather than IControlCore: since ControlCore became a
marker class, [default_interface] gave it its own class-specific
interface that only cascadia exposes. QI'ing for IControlCore would
succeed for every engine and hand the wrong type to get_self.

For a non-cascadia engine, OnCreateAutomationPeer returns null - which
TermControl::OnCreateAutomationPeer has always handled as "no custom
peer, use XAML's default" - and GetRenderData returns null. Screen-reader
support on a second engine is Phase 8's, per PLAN.

Two call sites needed adjusting beyond the mechanical -> to .:
- The paste lambda captured _core and dereferenced it.
- _mouseTransparencyHandler bound ControlCore's internal one-argument
  AdjustOpacity, which is the relative overload. Only the two-argument
  form is projected, so relative-ness is now explicit; same code runs.

The interactivity tests reach into cascadia internals (_terminal,
_inUnitTests), so their helper takes the concrete core through
_cascadiaCore() - which means every test run exercises that QI.

Tests: 295/295 (terminalCore 54, unitSettingsModel 158, unitControl 32,
terminalApp 51), matching the Phase 4 baseline.

